      "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${5}^{n}+{9}^{n}+2$ is divisible by $4$ for all $n\\in N.$\n\nLet $P(n)$ denote the following sequence of statements:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,4\\,∣\\,{5}^{n}+{9}^{n}+2\n$$\n\n We proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nSubstituting $n=\\,${{field-2}} into the expression ${5}^{n}+{9}^{n}+2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-3}}={{field-4}},\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=\\,${{field-7}}.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-8}}, we have that\n\n$$\nP(k):\\,{{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}+{{field-12}}+{5}^{k}+{9}^{k}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-13}} by the inductive hypothesis, and {{field-14}} and {{field-15}} we have that {{field-16}}, \nas required.",
